WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.011
2026: the benchmark’s co-creator stops using it.

2
00:00:04.291 --> 00:00:12.973
In February 2026, OpenAI published an audit of SWE-bench Verified and said it would no longer evaluate on it.

3
00:00:13.253 --> 00:00:22.192
The OpenAI post blocks automated fetching, so this lesson relies on Epoch AI’s benchmark review, which reports the audit’s findings.

4
00:00:22.472 --> 00:00:24.648
OpenAI audited 27.

5
00:00:24.928 --> 00:00:27.851
6% of the tasks and found that 59.

6
00:00:28.131 --> 00:00:38.349
4% of those had flawed tests that rejected functionally correct submissions, which puts a floor of about 16% on the share of all tasks that are broken.

7
00:00:38.629 --> 00:00:51.899
It also reported that every frontier model it tested had seen at least some of the problems and solutions during training, and concluded that gains increasingly reflected training exposure rather than real-world ability.

8
00:00:52.179 --> 00:00:55.592
OpenAI pointed to SWE-bench Pro instead.

9
00:00:55.872 --> 00:01:12.299
Epoch’s own review rates SWE-bench Verified as Flawed, citing the scoring errors, contamination risk (all tasks and solutions are public), heavy dependence on the scaffold used to run the model, and a reported reward hack that achieves a perfect score.

10
00:01:12.579 --> 00:01:14.861
None of this means past progress was fake.

11
00:01:15.141 --> 00:01:17.936
Models really did get much better at fixing issues.

12
00:01:18.216 --> 00:01:25.981
It means a public benchmark has a shelf life, and that a single headline number from it, in 2026, is weak evidence.
